- How would you test dead-letter routing under load?
- How would you tune retry/backoff for DLQ messages?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical observability and data reliability skills in a real-world, beginner-friendly setting.

## Key Concepts
- Beam Metrics and Cloud Monitoring integration
- Idempotent streaming writes with insertId
- Lightweight auditing and error handling
- Alerting on data quality metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-beam sketch (JavaScript/TypeScript syntax used for illustration)
class ParseEvent extends DoFn {
  @ProcessElement
  process(@Element json, output) {
    const event = JSON.parse(json.image);
    this.ingested.inc();
    // ... validation and transformation
    this.processed.inc();
    output(event);
  }

  @Setup
  setup() {
    this.ingested = Metrics.counter('pipeline', 'ingested_events');
    this.processed = Metrics.counter('pipeline', 'processed_events');
    this.failed = Metrics.counter('pipeline', 'failed_events');
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
 Tests ability to design a scalable, multi-tenant privacy enforcement and incident response workflow on GCP, including data lineage, per-tenant isolation, and automated remediation under real-time load.

## Key Concepts
 - Real-time data processing, per-tenant isolation, data masking, DLP, Vertex AI, Dataflow, Pub/Sub, Data Catalog lineage
 - Event-time vs processing-time semantics, idempotency, backpressure, rollback strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
function maskPII(record){
  const r = { ...record };
  if (r.ssn) r.ssn = '***-**-****';
  if (r.email) r.email = r.email.replace(/(.{1})(.*)(@)/, '$1***$2$3');
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the anomaly model under bursty traffic and schema drift?
- How would you handle false positives/negatives and post-incident cleanup?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a candidate's ability to implement a straightforward streaming ingestion with validation, error handling, and rollback using familiar GCP primitives.

## Key Concepts
- Pub/Sub ingestion and Dataflow (Beam)
- JSON schema validation and field filtering
- BigQueryIO writes with insertId for idempotency and daily partitioning
- Dead-letter handling and Cloud Storage archival
- Lightweight testing and rollback planning

## Code Example
```javascript
function validate(record){
  if (typeof record.user_id !== 'string') return false;
  if (typeof record.event_time !== 'string') return false;
  if (typeof record.event_type !== 'string') return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle late-arriving data and schema evolution?
- What monitoring and alerting would you add for bad records and rollback validation?

## Why This Is Asked
This angle explores multi-tenant isolation, masking, and rollback in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Dataflow (Beam) streaming pipelines
- Pub/Sub for ingestion
- Per-tenant BigQuery datasets
- Data Loss Prevention masking in ingest
- Idempotent writes with MERGE
- Dead-letter queues and raw data archiving
- 24h rollback via replay from Cloud Storage
- Observability through Dataflow metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Dataflow masking sketch (pseudo)
class MaskPII {
  process(element) {
    if (element.email) element.email = '*****@*****.com';
    if (element.phone) element.phone = '**********';
    return element;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data quality across tenants? 
- How would you enforce per-tenant quotas in BigQuery and Dataflow?
